- Tag each recipe page with the foods it contains where the food is a significant proportion of the meal.
- Tag the page with "Recipe" too, so that it appears in index.md.
- In the front matter, list_image: (image file for recipe)
- Tag with the main biological target tags.

## Meal category tagging (Drinks and Snacks)

- **Drinks in the `Drinks/` folder**: Tag with "Drinks" only. Do not add "Dinner", "Lunch", or other meal tags, so they appear only on the Drinks page and not on meal-specific pages (e.g. Dinner).
- **Meal-specific drinks** (e.g. breakfast smoothies): Place in the meal category folder (`Breakfast/`, `Lunch/`, etc.) with both the meal category tag AND "Drinks" tag.
- **General drinks** (e.g. Sourdough Soda, Turmeric Milk): Keep in `Drinks/` folder with only the "Drinks" tag.
- **Snacks in the `Snacks/` folder**: Tag with "Snacks" (and "Recipe"). They are listed from the Foods index and the `Snacks` recipes category page; do not use "Drinks" for snack-format recipes moved out of `Drinks/`.

The Format of the page should have this structure:

- Image of the recipe.
- Overview: A single paragraph description describing the recipe, what it is, where it comes from, why it's delicious and good for you. Keep it brief.
- Ingredients: a list of ingredients and amounts.
- Method: a numbered list of steps.
- Extra Guidance (optional): Any other notes for how or when to take it.
- Nutrition: calories, etc.
- Brain Health Notes: some notes about why it is particularly helpful for your brain.
- Foods/Substances: should be <RecipeFoods details={frontMatter} /> (displays foods with hyperlinked substances for each food)
- Biological Target Matrix: should be <RecipeMatrix details={frontMatter} />

Use the `Ginger Yogurt & Blueberry Bowl.md` page as an example.

## Ingredient Unit Standard (required)

All ingredient lines in `## Ingredients` must include **both**:

1. **Metric value** (`g`, `mg`, `µg`, `ml`, `l`)  
2. **Cooking equivalent** (e.g. `tsp`, `tbsp`, `cup`, `oz`) in parentheses when the culinary measure is not already grams

The prose list must match unique `display` strings in `recipe_ingredients`. Do not maintain two independent ingredient truths.

## Recipe nutrition (required rule)

**Never render a quantitative recipe nutrition table unless every default-included ingredient has both** an unambiguous food-composition record **and** a resolved edible quantity in grams (`calculation_weight_g`) with a recorded conversion source.

If those conditions are not met, the Foods/Substances block shows only:

> Detailed nutrition calculation pending ingredient-weight reconciliation.

Do **not** sum 100 g of each tagged food. See `system/recipe-ingredient-schema.md`.

Also required:

- A non-gram `unit` needs a `conversion_source` naming a USDA `food_portion` row, concordant FDC Branded serving declarations, a declared drained weight, or an explicit midpoint of a range the recipe states. Never equate millilitres with grams.
- Use `composition_ref` into `src/data/recipeCompositionSnapshots.mjs` when the ingredient has no food page, or when the food page records a different preparation state (uncooked quinoa for a cooked-quinoa line, raw corn for drained canned corn, lemon flesh for lemon juice).
- Every `included_in_default: false` line needs an `excluded_reason`. Salt added to taste is excluded and disclosed as `excluding salt added to taste`.
- A nutrient the sources cannot establish goes in `nutrition_unresolved` and renders as *Not established*, never as a number or a zero.
- If the recipe declares no serving count and one cannot be read from its own text, set `servings_unresolved: true`. The calculator follows declared servings; it never decides them.

The public block is a visible meal summary (Energy, Protein, Carbohydrate, Sugars, Fibre, Total fat, Saturated fat, Sodium) plus three collapsed sections: Key vitamins and minerals, Bioactive compounds, Calculation details.

## Biological Target Matrix (required rule)

**Never render a generated Biological Target Matrix** unless the recipe front matter sets `recipe_matrix_validated: true` after canonical BRS review.

If that flag is absent or false, show only:

> Biological Target Matrix pending canonical BRS validation.

Do not leave a tag-walked table public merely because it is labelled estimated or automatically generated. Do not set `recipe_matrix_validated` during routine recipe edits. See `system/recipe-matrix-schema-proposal.md`.

Canonical front matter:

```yaml
servings: 1
recipe_ingredients:
  - display: 150 g full-fat Greek yogurt
    food_slug: greek-yogurt
    quantity: 150
    unit: g
    calculation_weight_g: 150
    included_in_default: true
    composition_basis: named food-page record
    conversion_source: recipe-stated edible grams
```

Legacy `recipe_nutrition` with explicit `food` + `grams` is still calculated. It is not a 100 g fallback.

## Copy/Paste Recipe Template (canonical)

Use this as the baseline for new recipe pages so the parsing and UI components behave consistently.

```md
---
id: <recipe-id-slug>
title: <Human Title>
sidebar_label: <Human Title>
description: <One sentence: what it is + why it is good>
tags:
  - Recipe
  - <Meal category (Breakfast/Lunch/Dinner/Side-dishes/Drinks)>
  - <Food tags (must match food page titles)>
  - <Main biological target tags>
list_image: /img/recipes/<image-file>.jpg
---

<img src="/img/recipes/<image-file>.jpg" alt="<Alt text>" width="480" />

## Overview

_Replace with a single paragraph describing what the recipe is and why it is delicious and good for you._

## Ingredients (servings)

- _metric_ (_cooking equivalent_) _ingredient_
- _metric_ (_cooking equivalent_) _ingredient_

## Method

1. _step_
2. _step_

## Extra Guidance (optional)

- _note_

## Nutrition

_Replace with the calculated per-serving figures. Do not leave an untraced estimate that disagrees with the Recipe nutrition table._

## Brain Health Notes

- _why it helps the brain_

## Foods/Substances

<RecipeFoods details={frontMatter} />

## Biological Target Matrix

<RecipeMatrix details={frontMatter} />
```
